mcron
=====
A minimal, resource-efficient cron daemon written in pure C, with
anacron-style catch-up for systems with intermittent uptime.


Features
--------
* Zero Bloat: Standard C99, no dependencies beyond a POSIX libc.

* Catch-Up: Remembers the last processed minute. After downtime (power
  off, suspend, daemon restart) every job whose schedule fell into the gap
  runs once. A job is never run twice for the same minute.

* Clock-Jump Safe: If the clock is set back, nothing runs until time
  passes the recorded minute again; no duplicate runs and no task floods.

* Strict Parsing: Invalid lines are rejected and logged to syslog instead
  of being silently misread.

* Hardened: Config and state directory must be owned by the daemon's user
  and not group/world writable; state files are written atomically; a locked
  pid file prevents a second instance.


Dependencies
------------
A standard POSIX-compliant C compiler and make


Installation
------------
To compile the binary, install it, and set up the default configuration file:

    sudo make clean install

To customize the compiler, paths, or flags, edit `config.mk` before building.


Usage
-----
Edit `/etc/mcron.conf`:

    # min hour dom mon dow command
    0 4 * * 1 /usr/local/bin/maintenance.sh

Each field is `*` or a single number. Lists, ranges and steps are not
supported. The file must be owned by the user running mcron and must not be
writable by group or others.

    mcron       run in the background
    mcron -f    stay in the foreground (for runit and friends)
    mcron -v    print the version

For detailed documentation, refer to the man page:

    man mcron
